      "content": "Cut Set"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A cut set is the set of all the edges in a graph that must be removed to disconnect the graph."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "It represents the minimum set of edges needed to separate the graph into distinct components."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example, consider the following graph:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Cut Set in a Graph",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We can disconnect the graph by removing the three edges bd, be, and ce, but we cannot disconnect it by removing just one or two of these edges."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So, our cut-set is {bd, be, ce}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
